ice him well clear of even odds. Mackenzie's incumbency, fundraising edge, and the district's near-even lean keep this short of Likely D.", "watch": "A late shift among blue-collar voters toward Mackenzie, or new polls showing the race tied, would move this back toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ryan Mackenzie (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Ryan Mackenzie, who won this Lehigh Valley seat by the narrowest margin in Pennsylvania two years ago, faces Democrat Bob Brooks in a rematch-style contest on nearly even partisan ground. A September Franklin & Marshall poll shows Brooks ahead 45-40, and Democratic-sponsored polling from July showed a similar four-point edge. Mackenzie holds a clear fundraising advantage with about $2.9 million on hand, though outside groups have spent heavily to boost Brooks.\n\nExpert raters are split: several call the race a Tossup while others, including DDHQ, FPO, Split Ticket, and the Silver Bulletin, rate it a Democratic flip opportunity. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket, and PredictIt all price Brooks around 80 percent. With Trump's approval low and Democrats leading in Pennsylvania congressional polling, the race tilts to Brooks, but Mackenzie's incumbency and the district's swing nature keep it competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7234, "margin": 3.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7148, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7673, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7925, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7174, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7271, "margin": 4.2}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7743, "margin": 4.0}]}